  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 356 AM CDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nMid-level trough axis this morning is currently positioned across \nthe eastern Plains, and will continue to shift eastward with \nadditional digging toward the lower Missouri River Valley. Several \npockets of upper-level divergence has been providing extra lift \nacross much of the Upper Midwest and middle Mississippi River Valley \nover the past 12-18 hours. Surface cyclone has continued to deepen \nacross parts of the Central and Southern Plains, with surface \ntroughing extending northeastward into northern Missouri. This area \nof surface troughing is also where a stalled thermal boundary \ncurrently sits. And overnight convection in eastern Nebraska, \nnorthwest Missouri, and western Iowa has provided precipitation drag \non the cool side to further reinforce the temperature gradient \nassociated with it. This will set the stage for favorable forcing \nWednesday afternoon and evening for new development. For the \nremainder of the morning, a few weak mid-level vort maxima are set \nto track across the area, which may provide enough mid-level lift of \na warm and moisture boundary layer along and ahead of the frontal \nboundary to generate some shower activity. 06z HRRR and other CAMs \nhave been keeping morning activity more in northern Missouri to \nCentral Iowa, where stronger surface convergence has been present \nalong the boundary this morning. This area has also seen few mid-\nlevel disturbances that provided further lift, compared to areas \nsouth of Hwy. 36 this morning. The NAMNest is the only model that \ntries to generate stronger convection further south during the \nmorning hours, but current radar trends seem to support more of a \nHRRR and even RRFS solution. Wind shear, especially with the low-\nlevel jet could help organize convection this morning in our \nnorthern zones, but the better kinematic support for that is \ncurrently concentrated over Central Iowa. We will need to watch \nrainfall rates if anything does move southward, especially in Adair \nCounty MO and surrounding areas that saw locally heavy rainfall late \nTuesday evening. \n\nMost of the redevelopment and threat for severe weather on Wednesday \nafternoon and evening is going to heavily depend on seeing some \nclearing, and having enough boundary layer destabilization to get \nstronger storms going. We should see the surface cyclone move \nslightly eastward, with surface troughing and associated pressure \nfalls moving further into the lower Missouri River Valley. \nDifferential heating potential along the boundary will also play a \nrole in convergence. Stronger theta-e advection this afternoon and \nevening should help areas southeast of the thermal boundary reach \nMUCAPE values between 1500-2000 J/kg. Some solutions that completely \nclear out morning activity push SBCAPE values to  near 3500 J/kg. \nGetting activity to initiate with plenty of low-level and even mid-\nlevel kinematic support should not be a problem. And while \ndestabilization occurs and increasing CAPE values, lapse rates based \non model soundings on the warm side of the boundary are not overly \nsteep. Selecting a few points throughout the forecast area, we are \nnot seeing any lapse rates get much steeper than 6C/km. This may \nend up being the limiting factor to getting stronger cores this \nafternoon, despite 0-6km bulk shear values between 30-40 kts \n(potentially nearing 50kt later Wednesday evening in Central MO). \nWhile the kinematic parameters may initially look favorable for \nsupercell storm mode, the broadscale lift support by the approaching \n500mb vort maxima may lead to interference, yielding widespread \nmulticells through the afternoon. As for storm evolution, there \nlooks to be 3 distinct rounds of convection initiation. The first is \nalready occurring this morning in Iowa and northern Missouri. The \npotential for this to move southward across the rest of the forecast \narea appears to dwindling, though there are few light radar returns \nover central Kansas that will need to be monitored. After 18z today, \nwhen there will be some destabilization (the magnitude of it will \nlargely depend on any cloud clearing if we are able to get some \nsubsidence after any more rain showers). Multiple CAM solutions \ndevelop multi cells from eastern Kansas to Central Missouri. Based \non simulated radar reflectivity fields, the stronger cores between \n18-22z this afternoon are closer to Central MO. And this may be \nbecause those areas may be further removed from cloud cover that is \notherwise closer to the boundary, and perhaps larger surface \npressure fall drop through the afternoon. However, lapse rates from \nsoundings near the environment CI there are still not overly \nimpressive, and any cells that develop will have to tap into the \nshear to organize. With the moisture that is present (PWATs ranging \nbetween 1.75 to nearly 2.00 inches on the warm side of the \nboundary), precipitation loading may be enough to create stronger \nvertical pressure gradient pertu",
